# macOS: raw DNS and fetch in cua_repl fail with ENOTFOUND while shell HTTPS succeeds

## Environment

- Reproduced 2026-09-22.
- macOS 15.7.9, arm64.
- Desktop app and unified-computer-use plugin: 26.915.31945.
- Bundled Node: v24.21.0.
- Task declares workspace-write sandbox with network enabled.
- Plugin launches bundled `@oai/cua-repl/bin/cua-repl.mjs`, which launches bundled `node_repl`.
- `CUA_REPL_ENABLED_SURFACES=browser,computer`.
- Plugin manifest has `env_vars: []`; no explicit proxy variables in its env keys. Shell proxy variables were absent, and the system-proxy check returned no enabled indicators. This does not establish the absence of all network filtering.

## Minimal reproduction

Initialize the exposed CUA tool through its documented entry point, then execute the following inside `cua_repl` (not a shell Node process):

```js
var dns = await import('node:dns/promises');
var result = {};
try {
  result.dns = await dns.lookup('example.com');
} catch (e) {
  result.dns = { name: e.name, code: e.code, message: e.message };
}
try {
  var r = await fetch('https://example.com', {
    signal: AbortSignal.timeout(10000)
  });
  result.fetch = { ok: r.ok, status: r.status };
} catch (e) {
  result.fetch = { name: e.name, causeCode: e.cause?.code, message: e.message };
}
nodeRepl.write(result);
```

Actual result:

```json
{
  "dns": {"name":"Error","code":"ENOTFOUND","message":"getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND example.com"},
  "fetch": {"name":"TypeError","causeCode":"ENOTFOUND","message":"fetch failed"}
}
```

The same test against `api.typesafe.ai` also returns ENOTFOUND. No Jev import or API key is needed to reproduce this.

## Control observations

- CUA initialization/inventory succeeds; this is not a missing-tool failure.
- In the same task, shell curl to `https://example.com` returns HTTP 200.
- Shell curl to `https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone` returns HTTP 405, establishing HTTPS reachability, not API authentication or inference success.
- Task-scoped diagnostic records include ENOTFOUND. Scoped desktop-log and recent sandboxd checks did not expose an explicit denial explaining it; absence of a log match is not proof that no sandbox restriction exists.
- No launcher patches, security changes, proxy injection, or credential changes were made during this inspection.

## Question / expected behavior

Is arbitrary public DNS/HTTPS intentionally unsupported from user JavaScript in this CUA runtime? If so, please document the boundary and supported integration route. If permitted, this appears to need a runtime/network-policy diagnosis: enabling shell network access alone does not make it work.

This blocks a third-party Jev bridge that makes its provider request within cua_repl. Related proxy reports #22623 and #42454 may concern a different path; this report does not claim the same root cause or that a proxy workaround applies.

No private paths, credentials, raw logs, browser inventory, or task identifiers are included.
